{
  "version": 1,
  "id": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000302",
  "title": "Lagrange Points",
  "created_at": "2026-03-26T00:00:00Z",
  "updated_at": "2026-03-26T00:00:00Z",
  "shared_cargo_toml": "[dependencies]\nplotters = \"0.3\"\n\n[profile.release]\nopt-level = 1\nlto = false\ncodegen-units = 16",
  "shared_source": null,
  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "cell_0",
      "order": 0,
      "label": "Introduction",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "# Lagrange Points\n\nIn celestial mechanics, the **Lagrange points** are five positions in a two-body gravitational system where a small object can remain stationary relative to the two large bodies.\n\n## The Circular Restricted Three-Body Problem (CR3BP)\n\nWe work in a **co-rotating frame** that spins with the two primary bodies ($M_1$, $M_2$). In this frame, both primaries are stationary and the dynamics of a test particle are governed by an effective potential that combines gravity and centrifugal effects:\n\n$$\\Phi(x, y) = -\\frac{1-\\mu}{r_1} - \\frac{\\mu}{r_2} - \\frac{1}{2}(x^2 + y^2)$$\n\nwhere $\\mu = M_2 / (M_1 + M_2)$ is the mass ratio, and $r_1$, $r_2$ are distances to each primary.\n\n## The Five Points\n\n| Point | Location | Stability |\n|-------|----------|-----------|\n| **L1** | Between the two bodies | Unstable (saddle) |\n| **L2** | Beyond the smaller body | Unstable (saddle) |\n| **L3** | Beyond the larger body | Unstable (saddle) |\n| **L4** | 60° ahead in orbit | Stable (for $\\mu < 0.0385$) |\n| **L5** | 60° behind in orbit | Stable (for $\\mu < 0.0385$) |\n\nL4 and L5 form equilateral triangles with the two primaries — these are the famous **Trojan points** where Jupiter's Trojan asteroids collect.\n\nRun the cells below to compute and visualize all five points!",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_1",
      "order": 1,
      "label": "Compute Lagrange Points",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "// Compute all 5 Lagrange points for the CR3BP\n// We use the Sun-Earth mass ratio: mu ~ 3e-6\n// In the rotating frame, M1 is at (-mu, 0) and M2 is at (1-mu, 0)\n\nlet mu: f64 = 0.01; // exaggerated for visibility (Earth-Moon ~ 0.012)\n\n// Positions of the two primaries in the rotating frame\nlet x1 = -mu;       // larger body\nlet x2 = 1.0 - mu;  // smaller body\n\n// ── L1, L2, L3: Collinear points (solve quintic numerically) ──\n// We use Newton's method on the effective force along the x-axis.\n\n// Effective force along x-axis (Coriolis-free, y=0):\n// F(x) = x - (1-mu)*(x - x1)/|x - x1|^3 - mu*(x - x2)/|x - x2|^3\nfn fx(x: f64, mu: f64) -> f64 {\n    let x1 = -mu;\n    let x2 = 1.0 - mu;\n    let r1 = (x - x1).abs();\n    let r2 = (x - x2).abs();\n    x - (1.0 - mu) * (x - x1) / (r1 * r1 * r1) - mu * (x - x2) / (r2 * r2 * r2)\n}\n\nfn dfx(x: f64, mu: f64) -> f64 {\n    let x1 = -mu;\n    let x2 = 1.0 - mu;\n    let r1 = (x - x1).abs();\n    let r2 = (x - x2).abs();\n    1.0 + 2.0 * (1.0 - mu) / (r1 * r1 * r1) + 2.0 * mu / (r2 * r2 * r2)\n        - 3.0 * (1.0 - mu) * (x - x1) * (x - x1) / (r1 * r1 * r1 * r1 * r1)\n        - 3.0 * mu * (x - x2) * (x - x2) / (r2 * r2 * r2 * r2 * r2)\n}\n\nfn newton(mut x: f64, mu: f64, iters: usize) -> f64 {\n    for _ in 0..iters {\n        let f = fx(x, mu);\n        let df = dfx(x, mu);\n        if df.abs() < 1e-15 { break; }\n        x -= f / df;\n    }\n    x\n}\n\n// L1: between M1 and M2\nlet l1_x = newton(0.5 * (x1 + x2), mu, 200);\nlet l1 = (l1_x, 0.0_f64);\n\n// L2: beyond M2 (away from M1)\nlet l2_x = newton(x2 + 0.2, mu, 200);\nlet l2 = (l2_x, 0.0_f64);\n\n// L3: beyond M1 (opposite side from M2)\nlet l3_x = newton(x1 - 1.0, mu, 200);\nlet l3 = (l3_x, 0.0_f64);\n\n// ── L4, L5: Triangular points (equilateral triangles) ──\nlet l4 = (0.5 - mu, (3.0_f64).sqrt() / 2.0);\nlet l5 = (0.5 - mu, -(3.0_f64).sqrt() / 2.0);\n\nlet output = format!(\n    \"Mass ratio mu = {}\\n\\n\\\n     Primary M1 at ({:.6}, 0)\\n\\\n     Primary M2 at ({:.6}, 0)\\n\\n\\\n     L1 = ({:.6}, {:.6})  — between bodies\\n\\\n     L2 = ({:.6}, {:.6})  — beyond M2\\n\\\n     L3 = ({:.6}, {:.6})  — beyond M1\\n\\\n     L4 = ({:.6}, {:.6})  — leading triangle\\n\\\n     L5 = ({:.6}, {:.6})  — trailing triangle\",\n    mu, x1, x2,\n    l1.0, l1.1, l2.0, l2.1, l3.0, l3.1, l4.0, l4.1, l5.0, l5.1\n);\n\nCellOutput::text(output)",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_2",
      "order": 2,
      "label": "Effective Potential Contours",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "use plotters::prelude::*;\n\nlet mu: f64 = 0.01;\nlet x1 = -mu;\nlet x2 = 1.0 - mu;\n\n// Effective potential in the rotating frame\nfn potential(x: f64, y: f64, mu: f64) -> f64 {\n    let x1 = -mu;\n    let x2 = 1.0 - mu;\n    let r1 = ((x - x1).powi(2) + y * y).sqrt();\n    let r2 = ((x - x2).powi(2) + y * y).sqrt();\n    -(1.0 - mu) / r1 - mu / r2 - 0.5 * (x * x + y * y)\n}\n\n// Compute Lagrange points (same Newton solver as above)\nfn fx(x: f64, mu: f64) -> f64 {\n    let x1 = -mu;\n    let x2 = 1.0 - mu;\n    let r1 = (x - x1).abs();\n    let r2 = (x - x2).abs();\n    x - (1.0 - mu) * (x - x1) / (r1 * r1 * r1) - mu * (x - x2) / (r2 * r2 * r2)\n}\nfn dfx(x: f64, mu: f64) -> f64 {\n    let x1 = -mu;\n    let x2 = 1.0 - mu;\n    let r1 = (x - x1).abs();\n    let r2 = (x - x2).abs();\n    1.0 + 2.0 * (1.0 - mu) / (r1 * r1 * r1) + 2.0 * mu / (r2 * r2 * r2)\n        - 3.0 * (1.0 - mu) * (x - x1).powi(2) / r1.powi(5)\n        - 3.0 * mu * (x - x2).powi(2) / r2.powi(5)\n}\nfn newton(mut x: f64, mu: f64) -> f64 {\n    for _ in 0..200 {\n        let f = fx(x, mu);\n        let df = dfx(x, mu);\n        if df.abs() < 1e-15 { break; }\n        x -= f / df;\n    }\n    x\n}\n\nlet l1 = (newton(0.5 * (x1 + x2), mu), 0.0);\nlet l2 = (newton(x2 + 0.2, mu), 0.0);\nlet l3 = (newton(x1 - 1.0, mu), 0.0);\nlet l4 = (0.5 - mu, (3.0_f64).sqrt() / 2.0);\nlet l5 = (0.5 - mu, -(3.0_f64).sqrt() / 2.0);\nlet lpoints = [(l1, \"L1\"), (l2, \"L2\"), (l3, \"L3\"), (l4, \"L4\"), (l5, \"L5\")];\n\n// Compute potential on a grid\nlet res = 400;\nlet xmin = -1.8_f64;\nlet xmax = 2.2_f64;\nlet ymin = -1.5_f64;\nlet ymax = 1.5_f64;\n\nlet mut grid = vec![vec![0.0_f64; res]; res];\nlet mut vmin = f64::MAX;\nlet mut vmax = f64::MIN;\n\nfor iy in 0..res {\n    for ix in 0..res {\n        let gx = xmin + (xmax - xmin) * ix as f64 / (res - 1) as f64;\n        let gy = ymin + (ymax - ymin) * iy as f64 / (res - 1) as f64;\n        let v = potential(gx, gy, mu);\n        // Clamp near singularities\n        let v = v.max(-5.0);\n        grid[iy][ix] = v;\n        if v < vmin { vmin = v; }\n        if v > vmax { vmax = v; }\n    }\n}\n\n// Draw contour plot using colored rectangles\nlet mut svg_buf = String::new();\n{\n    let root = SVGBackend::with_string(&mut svg_buf, (900, 700))\n        .into_drawing_area();\n    root.fill(&RGBColor(10, 10, 20)).unwrap();\n\n    let mut chart = ChartBuilder::on(&root)\n        .caption(\"Effective Potential & Lagrange Points\", (\"sans-serif\", 22).into_font().color(&WHITE))\n        .margin(15)\n        .x_label_area_size(35)\n        .y_label_area_size(45)\n        .build_cartesian_2d(xmin..xmax, ymin..ymax)\n        .unwrap();\n\n    chart.configure_mesh()\n        .label_style((\"sans-serif\", 12).into_font().color(&RGBColor(150, 150, 150)))\n        .axis_style(RGBColor(50, 50, 70))\n        .draw()\n        .unwrap();\n\n    // Draw potential as colored pixels\n    let dx = (xmax - xmin) / res as f64;\n    let dy = (ymax - ymin) / res as f64;\n    for iy in 0..res {\n        for ix in 0..res {\n            let gx = xmin + (xmax - xmin) * ix as f64 / (res - 1) as f64;\n            let gy = ymin + (ymax - ymin) * iy as f64 / (res - 1) as f64;\n            let t = (grid[iy][ix] - vmin) / (vmax - vmin);\n            // Deep blue -> cyan -> yellow -> white colormap\n            let (r, g, b) = if t < 0.33 {\n                let s = t / 0.33;\n                (0, (80.0 * s) as u8, (120.0 + 135.0 * s) as u8)\n            } else if t < 0.66 {\n                let s = (t - 0.33) / 0.33;\n                ((255.0 * s) as u8, (80.0 + 175.0 * s) as u8, (255.0 - 55.0 * s) as u8)\n            } else {\n                let s = (t - 0.66) / 0.34;\n                (255, 255, (200.0 + 55.0 * s) as u8)\n            };\n            chart.draw_series(std::iter::once(\n                Rectangle::new(\n                    [(gx - dx / 2.0, gy - dy / 2.0), (gx + dx / 2.0, gy + dy / 2.0)],\n                    RGBColor(r, g, b).filled(),\n                ),\n            )).unwrap();\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Draw contour lines at the potential values of L1-L5\n    let contour_vals: Vec<f64> = lpoints.iter()\n        .map(|((lx, ly), _)| potential(*lx, *ly, mu))\n        .collect();\n\n    // Mark the primaries\n    chart.draw_series(std::iter::once(\n        Circle::new((x1, 0.0), 8, RGBColor(255, 220, 50).filled()),\n    )).unwrap();\n    chart.draw_series(std::iter::once(\n        Circle::new((x2, 0.0), 5, RGBColor(100, 180, 255).filled()),\n    )).unwrap();\n\n    // Mark Lagrange points\n    let lp_colors = [\n        RGBColor(255, 80, 80),   // L1 red\n        RGBColor(255, 140, 50),  // L2 orange\n        RGBColor(200, 80, 255),  // L3 purple\n        RGBColor(80, 255, 120),  // L4 green\n        RGBColor(80, 255, 120),  // L5 green\n    ];\n    for (i, ((lx, ly), label)) in lpoints.iter().enumerate() {\n        let color = lp_colors[i];\n        chart.draw_series(std::iter::once(\n            Circle::new((*lx, *ly), 5, color.filled()),\n        )).unwrap();\n        // Label offset\n        chart.draw_series(std::iter::once(\n            Text::new(label.to_string(), (*lx + 0.06, *ly + 0.06),\n                (\"sans-serif\", 16).into_font().color(&WHITE)),\n        )).unwrap();\n    }\n\n    // Legend for primaries\n    chart.draw_series(std::iter::once(\n        Text::new(\"M1\", (x1 + 0.06, 0.08),\n            (\"sans-serif\", 14).into_font().color(&RGBColor(255, 220, 50))),\n    )).unwrap();\n    chart.draw_series(std::iter::once(\n        Text::new(\"M2\", (x2 + 0.06, 0.08),\n            (\"sans-serif\", 14).into_font().color(&RGBColor(100, 180, 255))),\n    )).unwrap();\n\n    root.present().unwrap();\n}\n\nSvg(svg_buf)",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_3",
      "order": 3,
      "label": "Orbit Simulations",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## Test Particle Orbits Near Each Lagrange Point\n\nBelow we simulate a test particle placed near each Lagrange point with a small perturbation. The equations of motion in the co-rotating frame include the **Coriolis** and **centrifugal** pseudo-forces:\n\n$$\\ddot{x} - 2\\dot{y} = \\frac{\\partial \\Phi}{\\partial x}$$\n$$\\ddot{y} + 2\\dot{x} = \\frac{\\partial \\Phi}{\\partial y}$$\n\n- **L1, L2, L3** are unstable saddle points — the particle drifts away (but real missions use station-keeping to stay nearby).\n- **L4, L5** are stable for small mass ratios — the particle orbits in **tadpole** or **horseshoe** patterns around the equilibrium.",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_4",
      "order": 4,
      "label": "Simulate Orbits Near All 5 Points",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "use plotters::prelude::*;\n\nlet mu: f64 = 0.01;\nlet x1p = -mu;\nlet x2p = 1.0 - mu;\n\n// Equations of motion in the rotating frame\n// State: [x, y, vx, vy]\nfn derivs(s: &[f64; 4], mu: f64) -> [f64; 4] {\n    let x = s[0];\n    let y = s[1];\n    let vx = s[2];\n    let vy = s[3];\n    let x1 = -mu;\n    let x2 = 1.0 - mu;\n    let r1 = ((x - x1).powi(2) + y * y).sqrt();\n    let r2 = ((x - x2).powi(2) + y * y).sqrt();\n    let r1_3 = r1.powi(3);\n    let r2_3 = r2.powi(3);\n    let ax = x + 2.0 * vy - (1.0 - mu) * (x - x1) / r1_3 - mu * (x - x2) / r2_3;\n    let ay = y - 2.0 * vx - (1.0 - mu) * y / r1_3 - mu * y / r2_3;\n    [vx, vy, ax, ay]\n}\n\n// RK4 integrator\nfn rk4_step(s: &mut [f64; 4], dt: f64, mu: f64) {\n    let k1 = derivs(s, mu);\n    let mut s2 = *s;\n    for i in 0..4 { s2[i] = s[i] + 0.5 * dt * k1[i]; }\n    let k2 = derivs(&s2, mu);\n    let mut s3 = *s;\n    for i in 0..4 { s3[i] = s[i] + 0.5 * dt * k2[i]; }\n    let k3 = derivs(&s3, mu);\n    let mut s4 = *s;\n    for i in 0..4 { s4[i] = s[i] + dt * k3[i]; }\n    let k4 = derivs(&s4, mu);\n    for i in 0..4 {\n        s[i] += dt / 6.0 * (k1[i] + 2.0 * k2[i] + 2.0 * k3[i] + k4[i]);\n    }\n}\n\n// Compute L-points\nfn fx_eq(x: f64, mu: f64) -> f64 {\n    let x1 = -mu;\n    let x2 = 1.0 - mu;\n    let r1 = (x - x1).abs();\n    let r2 = (x - x2).abs();\n    x - (1.0 - mu) * (x - x1) / (r1 * r1 * r1) - mu * (x - x2) / (r2 * r2 * r2)\n}\nfn dfx_eq(x: f64, mu: f64) -> f64 {\n    let x1 = -mu;\n    let x2 = 1.0 - mu;\n    let r1 = (x - x1).abs();\n    let r2 = (x - x2).abs();\n    1.0 + 2.0 * (1.0 - mu) / r1.powi(3) + 2.0 * mu / r2.powi(3)\n        - 3.0 * (1.0 - mu) * (x - x1).powi(2) / r1.powi(5)\n        - 3.0 * mu * (x - x2).powi(2) / r2.powi(5)\n}\nfn solve(mut x: f64, mu: f64) -> f64 {\n    for _ in 0..200 {\n        let f = fx_eq(x, mu);\n        let df = dfx_eq(x, mu);\n        if df.abs() < 1e-15 { break; }\n        x -= f / df;\n    }\n    x\n}\n\nlet l1 = (solve(0.5 * (x1p + x2p), mu), 0.0_f64);\nlet l2 = (solve(x2p + 0.2, mu), 0.0_f64);\nlet l3 = (solve(x1p - 1.0, mu), 0.0_f64);\nlet l4 = (0.5 - mu, (3.0_f64).sqrt() / 2.0);\nlet l5 = (0.5 - mu, -(3.0_f64).sqrt() / 2.0);\n\n// Perturbation and simulation parameters per point\nlet configs: Vec<(&str, (f64, f64), f64, f64, usize, f64, f64)> = vec![\n    // (label, L-point, perturbation, dt, steps, vx0, vy0)\n    (\"L1\", l1, 0.005, 0.002, 8000,  0.0, 0.008),\n    (\"L2\", l2, 0.005, 0.002, 8000,  0.0, -0.008),\n    (\"L3\", l3, 0.005, 0.002, 12000, 0.0, 0.005),\n    (\"L4\", l4, 0.005, 0.001, 40000, 0.0, 0.0),\n    (\"L5\", l5, 0.005, 0.001, 40000, 0.0, 0.0),\n];\n\n// Simulate each\nlet mut all_trajectories: Vec<(&str, Vec<(f64, f64)>, (f64, f64))> = Vec::new();\n\nfor (label, (lx, ly), pert, dt, steps, vx0, vy0) in &configs {\n    let mut state: [f64; 4] = [lx + pert, *ly, *vx0, *vy0];\n    let mut traj = vec![(state[0], state[1])];\n    for _ in 0..*steps {\n        rk4_step(&mut state, *dt, mu);\n        traj.push((state[0], state[1]));\n    }\n    all_trajectories.push((label, traj, (*lx, *ly)));\n}\n\n// Plot all 5 in a single figure with subplots (2x3 grid, last slot for legend)\nlet mut svg_buf = String::new();\n{\n    let root = SVGBackend::with_string(&mut svg_buf, (1100, 750))\n        .into_drawing_area();\n    root.fill(&RGBColor(10, 10, 20)).unwrap();\n\n    let title_style = (\"sans-serif\", 22).into_font().color(&WHITE);\n    root.titled(\"Test Particle Orbits Near Lagrange Points\", title_style).unwrap();\n\n    let areas = root.split_evenly((2, 3));\n\n    let colors = [\n        RGBColor(255, 80, 80),   // L1\n        RGBColor(255, 160, 50),  // L2\n        RGBColor(200, 80, 255),  // L3\n        RGBColor(80, 255, 120),  // L4\n        RGBColor(50, 200, 255),  // L5\n    ];\n\n    for (i, (label, traj, (lx, ly))) in all_trajectories.iter().enumerate() {\n        if i >= 5 { break; }\n        let area = &areas[i];\n\n        // Compute bounds around the L-point\n        let mut min_x = f64::MAX;\n        let mut max_x = f64::MIN;\n        let mut min_y = f64::MAX;\n        let mut max_y = f64::MIN;\n        for &(tx, ty) in traj {\n            if tx < min_x { min_x = tx; }\n            if tx > max_x { max_x = tx; }\n            if ty < min_y { min_y = ty; }\n            if ty > max_y { max_y = ty; }\n        }\n        let pad_x = (max_x - min_x) * 0.15 + 0.01;\n        let pad_y = (max_y - min_y) * 0.15 + 0.01;\n        min_x -= pad_x; max_x += pad_x;\n        min_y -= pad_y; max_y += pad_y;\n\n        let mut chart = ChartBuilder::on(area)\n            .caption(format!(\"{}\", label), (\"sans-serif\", 18).into_font().color(&WHITE))\n            .margin(8)\n            .x_label_area_size(25)\n            .y_label_area_size(40)\n            .build_cartesian_2d(min_x..max_x, min_y..max_y)\n            .unwrap();\n\n        chart.configure_mesh()\n            .label_style((\"sans-serif\", 10).into_font().color(&RGBColor(120, 120, 120)))\n            .axis_style(RGBColor(40, 40, 60))\n            .draw()\n            .unwrap();\n\n        // Draw trajectory\n        chart.draw_series(LineSeries::new(\n            traj.iter().copied(),\n            colors[i].stroke_width(1),\n        )).unwrap();\n\n        // Mark L-point\n        chart.draw_series(std::iter::once(\n            Circle::new((*lx, *ly), 4, WHITE.filled()),\n        )).unwrap();\n\n        // Mark start position\n        chart.draw_series(std::iter::once(\n            Circle::new(traj[0], 3, colors[i].filled()),\n        )).unwrap();\n    }\n\n    // Use the 6th panel for a legend / key\n    let info_area = &areas[5];\n    let mut info_chart = ChartBuilder::on(info_area)\n        .caption(\"Key\", (\"sans-serif\", 18).into_font().color(&WHITE))\n        .margin(15)\n        .build_cartesian_2d(0.0..1.0, 0.0..1.0)\n        .unwrap();\n\n    let labels = [\n        (0.7, \"L1 — Between bodies\", colors[0]),\n        (0.58, \"L2 — Beyond M2\", colors[1]),\n        (0.46, \"L3 — Beyond M1\", colors[2]),\n        (0.34, \"L4 — Leading triangle\", colors[3]),\n        (0.22, \"L5 — Trailing triangle\", colors[4]),\n    ];\n    for (ypos, text, color) in &labels {\n        info_chart.draw_series(std::iter::once(\n            Rectangle::new([(0.05, *ypos - 0.02), (0.15, *ypos + 0.02)], color.filled()),\n        )).unwrap();\n        info_chart.draw_series(std::iter::once(\n            Text::new(text.to_string(), (0.2, *ypos - 0.02),\n                (\"sans-serif\", 13).into_font().color(&WHITE)),\n        )).unwrap();\n    }\n    info_chart.draw_series(std::iter::once(\n        Circle::new((0.1, 0.82), 4, WHITE.filled()),\n    )).unwrap();\n    info_chart.draw_series(std::iter::once(\n        Text::new(\"= Lagrange point\", (0.2, 0.8),\n            (\"sans-serif\", 13).into_font().color(&WHITE)),\n    )).unwrap();\n\n    root.present().unwrap();\n}\n\nSvg(svg_buf)",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_5",
      "order": 5,
      "label": "Real-World Applications",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## Real-World Applications\n\n### L1: Solar Observation\nNASA's **SOHO** and **DSCOVR** spacecraft sit near the Sun-Earth L1 point, providing uninterrupted views of the Sun and early warning of solar storms.\n\n### L2: Deep Space Telescopes\nThe **James Webb Space Telescope** orbits Sun-Earth L2, where the Sun, Earth, and Moon are all behind its sunshield — ideal for infrared astronomy. **Euclid** and **Gaia** also operate here.\n\n### L3: Science Fiction Favorite\nThe Sun-Earth L3 point (on the far side of the Sun) is a staple of sci-fi as the hiding place for a \"Counter-Earth.\" In reality, L3 is unstable and gravitational perturbations from other planets make long-term occupation impractical.\n\n### L4 & L5: Trojan Asteroids\nJupiter's L4 and L5 points harbor over **12,000 known Trojan asteroids**. NASA's **Lucy** mission is currently visiting several of these ancient objects. The Sun-Earth L4/L5 regions have also been proposed as sites for future space colonies (the **O'Neill cylinder** concept).\n\n### Station-Keeping\nThe collinear points (L1, L2, L3) are all **unstable** — spacecraft must perform periodic thruster burns to remain nearby. They typically follow **Lissajous** or **halo orbits** around the Lagrange point rather than sitting exactly on it.\n\nThe triangular points (L4, L5) are **linearly stable** when the mass ratio satisfies $\\mu < \\mu_{\\text{crit}} \\approx 0.0385$ (the Gascheau/Routh criterion). This covers all planet-Sun pairs in our solar system.",
      "cargo_toml": null
    }
  ],
  "description": "Compute and visualize the five Lagrange points in the circular restricted three-body problem.",
  "tags": [
    "physics",
    "simulation",
    "orbital mechanics",
    "visualization"
  ]
}